select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 6, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.0", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.0 Overview", "body": "Carter, D. A., Rogers, D. A., and Simkins, B. J. (2006). Does hedging affect firm value? Evidence from the US airline industry. Financial Management, 35(1), 53–86.\nEderington, L. H. (1979). The hedging performance of the new futures markets. Journal of Finance, 34(1), 157–170.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 3.\nJohnson, L. L. (1960). The theory of hedging and speculation in commodity futures. Review of Economic Studies, 27(3), 139–151.\nStulz, R. M. (1996). Rethinking risk management. Journal of Applied Corporate Finance, 9(3), 8–25."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.0", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow many interest rate futures fix the cost of Northwind's loan?\nIn three months Northwind Components will borrow $20,000,000 for three months at the reference rate then prevailing. The futures market currently implies a rate of 4.40 percent for that period. A futures contract on the three-month rate is available: for each contract sold, the seller gains $25 for every 0.01 percentage point by which the rate rises above 4.40 percent, and loses $25 for every 0.01 point by which it falls below. Select the number of contracts to sell.\nThe cost is fixed with 20 contracts, one for each million dollars borrowed. The correspondence is not accidental: the contract is designed so that $25 is the interest on $1,000,000 for one quarter at 0.01 percent. Sections 6.2 and 6.3 present the two instruments, one over-the-counter and one exchange-traded, with which a future interest rate is fixed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.1", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.1", "heading": "Day count and quotation conventions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.1 Day count and quotation conventions", "body": "Interest accrues over time, and a convention is needed for measuring the time between two dates. A day count convention is written \\(X/Y\\), where \\(X\\) defines how the number of days between two dates is counted and \\(Y\\) defines the number of days in the reference period. The interest earned between two dates is\n\\[ \\frac{\\text{number of days between the dates}}{\\text{number of days in the reference period}}\\times\\text{interest earned in the reference period}. \\]\nTable 6.1 Principal day count conventions\nConventionDays between datesReference periodTypical use\nActual/actualActual calendar daysActual days in the coupon periodGovernment bonds in the United States and most other markets\n30/360Each month counted as 30 days360 days per yearCorporate and municipal bonds in the United States\nActual/360Actual calendar days360 days per yearMoney market instruments and floating rates in dollars and euros\nActual/365Actual calendar days365 days per yearMoney market instruments and floating rates in sterling"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.1", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.1", "heading": "Day count and quotation conventions", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.1 Day count and quotation conventions", "body": "Under actual/360, interest for a full year of 365 days is \\(365/360\\) times the quoted rate; a quoted rate of 4.40 percent produces 4.461 percent over a year. The difference matters in large transactions."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.1", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.1", "heading": "Day count and quotation conventions", "kind": "example", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.1 Day count and quotation conventions", "body": "Worked example 6.1 — Accrued interest under two conventions\nA bond with a principal of $100 pays a coupon of 6 percent per year on 1 March and 1 September. Find the interest accrued from 1 March to 3 July under (a) actual/actual and (b) 30/360.\n1\n(a) There are 184 actual days from 1 March to 1 September and 124 from 1 March to 3 July. Accrued interest: \\((124/184)\\times3.00=\\$2.0217\\).\n2\n(b) With 30-day months there are 180 days in the coupon period and \\(4\\times30+2=122\\) days from 1 March to 3 July. Accrued interest: \\((122/180)\\times3.00=\\$2.0333\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.1#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.1", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.1", "heading": "Clean and dirty prices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.1 Clean and dirty prices", "body": "The quoted price of a bond, called the clean price, excludes interest accrued since the last coupon. The amount a buyer actually pays, called the dirty price or cash price, is the quoted price plus accrued interest. The convention prevents the quoted price from falling abruptly each time a coupon is paid. Prices of United States Treasury bonds are quoted per $100 of principal in dollars and thirty-seconds of a dollar; a quotation of 104-16 means \\(104\\tfrac{16}{32}=\\$104.50\\). For clarity this chapter states prices in decimals."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Forward rate agreements", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Forward rate agreements", "body": "A forward rate agreement (FRA) is an over-the-counter contract that fixes the interest rate to apply to a specified principal for a specified future period. One party agrees to pay a fixed rate \\(R_K\\) and to receive the reference rate \\(R_M\\) that is observed in the market for the period; the other party takes the opposite side. No principal is exchanged. Only the difference in interest is paid.\nLet the period run from \\(T_1\\) to \\(T_2\\), let \\(L\\) be the principal, and let \\(\\tau=T_2-T_1\\). Both rates are expressed with a compounding period equal to \\(\\tau\\), so that interest for the period is the rate multiplied by \\(\\tau\\). For the party that pays fixed and receives the reference rate, the payoff valued at \\(T_2\\) is\n\\[ L\\,(R_M-R_K)\\,\\tau . \\]\nThis party gains when rates rise and is therefore the natural position for a future borrower; the opposite position suits a future lender or investor. An FRA is usually settled at \\(T_1\\), as soon as the reference rate is known, by payment of the present value of this amount, \\(L(R_M-R_K)\\tau/(1+R_M\\tau)\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Valuation", "body": "Section 3.5 showed that the forward rate \\(R_F\\) for a future period can be secured today at no cost. An FRA in which the fixed rate equals the forward rate therefore has zero value, and this is the rate at which FRAs are entered. For an existing FRA with fixed rate \\(R_K\\), the value is found by supposing that the reference rate will equal the forward rate and discounting the resulting payoff. With \\(R_2\\) the continuously compounded zero rate for maturity \\(T_2\\):\nResult 6.1 — Value of a forward rate agreement\nTo the party that pays the fixed rate \\(R_K\\) and receives the reference rate, \\[ V=L\\,(R_F-R_K)\\,\\tau\\,e^{-R_2T_2}, \\] where \\(R_F\\) is the forward rate for the period, expressed with compounding period \\(\\tau\\). To the other party the value is \\(-V\\).\nThe procedure — assume that forward rates are realized, then discount at the zero rates — is used again in Chapter 7 to value swaps. It does not assert that forward rates are forecasts. It is valid because each uncertain payment can be exchanged today, at no cost, for the payment computed at the forward rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "example", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Valuation", "body": "Worked example 6.2 — Northwind's forward rate agreement\nNorthwind will borrow $20,000,000 for three months, beginning in six months. It has entered an FRA to pay 4.60 percent (quarterly compounding) on that principal for that period. Zero rates with continuous compounding are 4.20 percent for six months and 4.40 percent for nine months. (a) Find the forward rate and the value of the FRA today. (b) If the reference rate for the period proves to be 5.30 percent, find the settlement amount and Northwind's net cost of borrowing.\n1\nForward rate, continuous compounding. \\(R_F=(4.40\\times0.75-4.20\\times0.50)/0.25=4.800\\%\\).\n2\nConverted to quarterly compounding. \\(4(e^{0.048/4}-1)=4.829\\%\\).\n3\nValue. \\(V=20{,}000{,}000\\times(0.04829-0.04600)\\times0.25\\times e^{-0.044\\times0.75}=11{,}446\\times0.967539=\\$11{,}074\\). The FRA has positive value to Northwind because the market's forward rate is now above the rate Northwind fixed.\n4\n(b) Settlement. Payoff valued at the end of the period: \\(20{,}000{,}000\\times(0.0530-0.0460)\\times0.25=\\$35{,}000\\). Paid at the start of the period: \\(35{,}000/(1+0.0530\\times0.25)=\\$34{,}542\\), received by Northwind.\n5"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "example", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Valuation", "body": "Net cost. Interest on the loan at 5.30 percent: \\(20{,}000{,}000\\times0.0530\\times0.25=\\$265{,}000\\). Less the payoff of the FRA: \\(265{,}000-35{,}000=\\$230{,}000\\), which is interest at 4.60 percent. The rate has been fixed."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Valuation", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 6.1 — Settlement of a forward rate agreement\nThe borrowing rate is fixed whatever the reference rate proves to be\nSet the fixed rate of the FRA and the reference rate observed at the start of the period. The table shows the payoff, the amount actually settled, and the cost of the loan net of the FRA."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.2#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.2", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.2", "heading": "Valuation", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.2 Valuation", "body": "Concept check 6.1\nA pension fund will receive $50,000,000 in four months and will invest it for six months. It wishes to fix the rate it will earn. Which position in an FRA is appropriate, and what happens if rates fall?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.3", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.3", "heading": "Futures on short-term interest rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.3 Futures on short-term interest rates", "body": "The exchange-traded counterpart of the FRA is the short-term interest rate futures contract. For three decades the principal contract was the Eurodollar futures contract of the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, which was based on three-month dollar LIBOR. With the discontinuation of LIBOR it has been replaced by the three-month SOFR futures contract, and contracts of the same design are traded on SONIA in London and on €STR in Frankfurt.\nThe contract has four features.\nReference period. Each contract refers to a quarter that begins on the third Wednesday of March, June, September, or December. The contract is named for the month in which its reference quarter begins and ceases trading at the end of that quarter.\nFinal settlement. At the end of the reference quarter the contract is settled in cash at \\(100-R\\), where \\(R\\) is the overnight SOFR rate compounded daily over the quarter, expressed as an annual percentage rate.\nQuotation. Before final settlement, the futures price is quoted as \\(100-R\\), where \\(R\\) is now the rate that the market implies for the reference quarter. A quotation of 95.60 implies a rate of 4.40 percent. The price therefore falls when rates rise."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.3", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.3", "heading": "Futures on short-term interest rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.3 Futures on short-term interest rates", "body": "Contract value. One basis point, defined as 0.01 percentage point, is worth $25 per contract. This is the interest on $1,000,000 for one quarter at 0.01 percent: \\(1{,}000{,}000\\times0.0001\\times0.25=\\$25\\).\nBecause the price is \\(100-R\\), a short position gains when rates rise and a long position gains when rates fall. A future borrower, who is harmed by a rise in rates, hedges by selling contracts; a future lender hedges by buying them. The number of contracts required to hedge a three-month exposure is the principal divided by $1,000,000."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.3", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.3", "heading": "Futures on short-term interest rates", "kind": "example", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.3 Futures on short-term interest rates", "body": "Worked example 6.3 — Fixing a borrowing rate with SOFR futures\nNorthwind will borrow $20,000,000 for the quarter beginning on the third Wednesday of June, at compounded SOFR. The June three-month SOFR futures price is 95.60. Northwind sells 20 contracts. Compounded SOFR for the quarter proves to be 5.10 percent. Find the result.\n1\nRate fixed. \\(100-95.60=4.40\\) percent.\n2\nFutures. Final settlement price: \\(100-5.10=94.90\\). The price has fallen by 0.70, or 70 basis points. Gain on the short position: \\(70\\times25\\times20=\\$35{,}000\\).\n3\nLoan. Interest at 5.10 percent: \\(20{,}000{,}000\\times0.0510\\times0.25=\\$255{,}000\\).\n4\nNet cost. \\(255{,}000-35{,}000=\\$220{,}000\\), which is interest at 4.40 percent: \\(20{,}000{,}000\\times0.0440\\times0.25\\). For simplicity the calculation treats the quarter as exactly one quarter of a year; in practice interest accrues on an actual/360 basis."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.3#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.3", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.3", "heading": "Futures on short-term interest rates", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.3 Futures on short-term interest rates", "body": "★ Optional: the convexity adjustment. The rate implied by a futures price is slightly higher than the forward rate for the same period. The futures contract is settled daily, and a party that is short gains when rates rise, when the gain can be invested at high rates, and loses when rates fall, when the loss can be financed cheaply. A short position in futures is therefore more favorable than the corresponding position in an FRA, and the futures rate must be higher to compensate. A standard approximation is \\[ \\text{Forward rate}=\\text{Futures rate}-\\tfrac12\\sigma^{2}T_1T_2, \\] where \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) are the times to the start and end of the reference period and \\(\\sigma\\) is the standard deviation of the change in the short-term interest rate in one year, with both rates expressed with continuous compounding. With \\(\\sigma=0.012\\), \\(T_1=4\\), and \\(T_2=4.25\\), the adjustment is \\(0.5\\times0.012^{2}\\times4\\times4.25=0.00122\\), or about 12 basis points. For contracts maturing within a year it is less than one basis point and is usually ignored."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.4", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.4", "heading": "Treasury bond futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.4 Treasury bond futures", "body": "The principal contracts on long-term interest rates are futures on government bonds: Treasury bond and Treasury note futures in Chicago, and contracts on German, British, and Japanese government bonds on their respective exchanges. The United States Treasury bond futures contract is described here; the others are similar in design.\nThe contract is on $100,000 of principal. The party with the short position may deliver any Treasury bond with between 15 and 25 years remaining to maturity. Since the eligible bonds differ in coupon and maturity, and therefore in price, the exchange adjusts the amount paid according to the bond delivered, by means of a conversion factor published for each bond. The conversion factor is approximately the price, per dollar of principal, that the bond would have if its yield were 6 percent with semiannual compounding. The cash received by the party making delivery is\n\\[ (\\text{settlement futures price}\\times\\text{conversion factor})+\\text{accrued interest}, \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.4", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.4", "heading": "Treasury bond futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.4 Treasury bond futures", "body": "per $100 of principal. If the settlement price is 112.00, the conversion factor of the bond delivered is 0.9300, and accrued interest is $1.25, the cash received is \\(112.00\\times0.9300+1.25=\\$105.41\\) per $100 of principal, or $105,410 for one contract."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.4", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.4", "heading": "The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.4 The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "body": "The conversion factor system does not make all bonds equally attractive to deliver. The party with the short position will choose the bond for which the cost of acquiring it, less the amount received for delivering it, is least. Accrued interest is both paid on purchase and received on delivery and so cancels. The bond chosen, the cheapest-to-deliver bond, is the one that minimizes\n\\[ \\text{quoted bond price}-(\\text{settlement futures price}\\times\\text{conversion factor}). \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.4", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.4", "heading": "The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "kind": "example", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.4 The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "body": "Worked example 6.4 — Cheapest to deliver\nThe settlement futures price is 112.00. Three bonds may be delivered: bond A, quoted at 118.50 with a conversion factor of 1.0500; bond B, quoted at 135.25 with a factor of 1.2000; and bond C, quoted at 104.75 with a factor of 0.9300. Which is cheapest to deliver?\n1\nBond A. \\(118.50-112.00\\times1.0500=118.50-117.60=0.90\\).\n2\nBond B. \\(135.25-112.00\\times1.2000=135.25-134.40=0.85\\).\n3\nBond C. \\(104.75-112.00\\times0.9300=104.75-104.16=0.59\\).\n4\nConclusion. Bond C is cheapest to deliver. The futures price is determined by this bond, and the duration of the futures contract, required in the next section, is taken to be the duration of this bond at the maturity of the contract."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.4#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.4", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.4", "heading": "The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.4 The cheapest-to-deliver bond", "body": "Which bond is cheapest to deliver depends on the level of yields. When yields are above 6 percent, the conversion factor system favors the delivery of bonds with low coupons and long maturities; when yields are below 6 percent, it favors bonds with high coupons and short maturities. The party with the short position also has some choice over the timing of delivery within the delivery month. These options are valuable to the short position, and the futures price is correspondingly a little lower than it would be without them."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.5#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.5", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.5", "heading": "Duration-based hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.5 Duration-based hedging", "body": "Section 3.7 established that the change in the value of a bond for a small change \\(\\Delta y\\) in its yield is approximately \\(-BD\\,\\Delta y\\). Consider a portfolio of interest-sensitive assets with value \\(V_P\\) and duration \\(D_P\\) at the date the hedge is to be lifted, and a futures contract with value \\(V_F\\) and duration \\(D_F\\), the duration of the asset underlying the futures at the maturity of the contract. If all yields change by the same amount, \\(\\Delta V_P\\approx-V_PD_P\\,\\Delta y\\) and \\(\\Delta V_F\\approx-V_FD_F\\,\\Delta y\\). The number of contracts that makes the combined position insensitive to \\(\\Delta y\\) is the duration-based hedge ratio:\nResult 6.2 — Duration-based hedge ratio\n\\[ N^{*}=\\frac{V_P\\,D_P}{V_F\\,D_F}. \\] A holder of the portfolio hedges with a short position of \\(N^{*}\\) contracts; a party that will acquire the portfolio hedges with a long position."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.5#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.5", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.5", "heading": "Duration-based hedging", "kind": "example", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.5 Duration-based hedging", "body": "Worked example 6.5 — Hedging a bond portfolio\nA fund holds $10,000,000 of bonds with a duration of 6.8 years and wishes to protect their value over the next three months. Treasury bond futures are quoted at 110.00, and the cheapest-to-deliver bond will have a duration of 8.5 years at the maturity of the contract. Find the hedge, and verify it for a rise of 0.50 percentage points in all yields.\n1\nContract value. \\(V_F=110.00\\times1{,}000=\\$110{,}000\\).\n2\nNumber of contracts. \\(N^{*}=(10{,}000{,}000\\times6.8)/(110{,}000\\times8.5)=72.7\\). The fund sells 73 contracts.\n3\nPortfolio. \\(\\Delta V_P\\approx-10{,}000{,}000\\times6.8\\times0.005=-\\$340{,}000\\).\n4\nFutures. Each contract falls in value by \\(110{,}000\\times8.5\\times0.005=\\$4{,}675\\). Gain on 73 short contracts: \\(73\\times4{,}675=\\$341{,}275\\).\n5\nNet. \\(-340{,}000+341{,}275=+\\$1{,}275\\), the effect of rounding to a whole number of contracts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.5#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.5", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.5", "heading": "Duration-based hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.5 Duration-based hedging", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 6.2 — The limits of a duration hedge\nA duration hedge protects against parallel shifts only\nThe first control changes the yield of the portfolio. The second sets the amount by which the yield underlying the futures changes by more or less than the yield of the portfolio, as happens when the yield curve changes shape. With the second control at zero the hedge of Worked example 6.5 holds; move it and observe the result."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.5#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.5", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.5", "heading": "Duration-based hedging", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.5 Duration-based hedging", "body": "The result rests on the assumption that the yield of the portfolio and the yield of the bond underlying the futures change by the same amount, a parallel shift of the yield curve. Short-term and long-term rates do not in fact move one for one, and a portfolio with a duration of 2 years hedged with a contract whose underlying bond has a duration of 8.5 years is exposed to any change in the difference between two-year and long-term yields. Two practices reduce the exposure: choosing a futures contract whose underlying duration is close to that of the portfolio, and hedging the exposure in each maturity range separately. The approximation also deteriorates for large changes in yield, when convexity becomes important.\nConcept check 6.2\nA three-month SOFR futures contract is quoted at 96.25. A trader buys 10 contracts, and the quotation moves to 96.05. What is the result?\nConcept check 6.3\nAn insurer will receive $25,000,000 in two months and will invest it in bonds with a duration of 9 years. It fears that yields will fall before then. Bond futures have a contract value of $125,000 and a duration of 7.5 years. What position is appropriate?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:6.5#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:6.5", "chapter": 6, "section": "6.5", "heading": "Duration-based hedging", "kind": "notes", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — 6.5 Duration-based hedging", "body": "Historical note\nInterest rate futures were first traded in October 1975, when the Chicago Board of Trade introduced a contract on mortgage-backed certificates of the Government National Mortgage Association. Treasury bond futures followed in 1977 and became for many years the most actively traded futures contract in the world.\nThe Eurodollar futures contract, introduced by the Chicago Mercantile Exchange in 1981, was the first major contract to be settled in cash, since a three-month interbank deposit could not conveniently be delivered. At its height it had open interest of more than ten million contracts. Trading in its successor, the three-month SOFR contract, began in 2018, and in April 2023 the exchange converted the remaining Eurodollar positions into SOFR contracts."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Summary#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Summary", "chapter": 6, "section": "Summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — Summary Summary", "body": "Interest between two dates is computed under a day count convention; a bond's cash price is its quoted price plus accrued interest.\nA forward rate agreement fixes the rate on a principal for a future period. The payer of the fixed rate receives \\(L(R_M-R_K)\\tau\\) and is protected against a rise in rates. The value of an FRA is found by assuming that the forward rate is realized and discounting.\nA three-month SOFR futures contract is quoted at 100 less the implied rate, and one basis point is worth $25. Borrowers sell contracts and lenders buy them, one contract for each $1,000,000 of a three-month exposure.\nIn a Treasury bond futures contract the short position chooses which bond to deliver and receives the settlement price multiplied by the bond's conversion factor, plus accrued interest. The cheapest-to-deliver bond minimizes the quoted price less the settlement price times the conversion factor.\nThe duration-based hedge ratio is \\(N^{*}=V_PD_P/(V_FD_F)\\). It protects against parallel shifts of the yield curve and not against changes in its shape."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 6, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — Exercises Exercises", "body": "6.1 A deposit of $5,000,000 earns 4.80 percent on an actual/360 basis for 91 days. Find the interest. What rate on an actual/365 basis would give the same interest?\nInterest: \\(5{,}000{,}000\\times0.048\\times91/360=\\$60{,}667\\). Equivalent actual/365 rate: \\(4.80\\times365/360=4.867\\) percent.\n6.2 A Treasury bond with a coupon of 5 percent is quoted at 97.25. Coupons are paid on 15 February and 15 August. Find the cash price per $100 of principal on 15 May, on an actual/actual basis. (There are 181 days from 15 February to 15 August and 89 from 15 February to 15 May.)\nAccrued interest: \\((89/181)\\times2.50=\\$1.2293\\). Cash price: \\(97.25+1.2293=\\$98.48\\).\n6.3 Zero rates with continuous compounding are 3.80 percent for one year and 4.10 percent for 1.5 years. A company has an FRA under which it receives a fixed rate of 5.00 percent with semiannual compounding on $10,000,000 for the six months beginning in one year. Find the value of the FRA to the company."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 6, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — Exercises Exercises", "body": "Forward rate: \\((4.10\\times1.5-3.80\\times1.0)/0.5=4.70\\) percent continuous, or \\(2(e^{0.0235}-1)=4.756\\) percent semiannual. The company receives fixed, so \\(V=10{,}000{,}000\\times(0.05000-0.04756)\\times0.5\\times e^{-0.041\\times1.5}=12{,}217\\times0.940353=\\$11{,}488\\).\n6.4 In Exercise 6.3, the reference rate for the period proves to be 4.20 percent with semiannual compounding. What payment is made at the start of the period, and by whom?\nPayoff to the receiver of fixed, valued at the end of the period: \\(10{,}000{,}000\\times(0.0500-0.0420)\\times0.5=\\$40{,}000\\). Settled at the start: \\(40{,}000/(1+0.0420\\times0.5)=\\$39{,}177\\), paid to the company by its counterparty.\n6.5 A bank will lend $45,000,000 for the quarter beginning in September at compounded SOFR. September three-month SOFR futures are quoted at 96.10. (a) What position fixes the bank's return, and at what rate? (b) If compounded SOFR for the quarter is 3.25 percent, verify the result."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 6, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — Exercises Exercises", "body": "(a) The bank is a lender and is harmed by a fall in rates; it buys 45 contracts, fixing \\(100-96.10=3.90\\) percent. (b) Final settlement: 96.75; the price has risen by 65 basis points. Gain: \\(65\\times25\\times45=\\$73{,}125\\). Interest earned: \\(45{,}000{,}000\\times0.0325\\times0.25=\\$365{,}625\\). Total: $438,750, which is \\(45{,}000{,}000\\times0.0390\\times0.25\\).\n6.6 The settlement price of a Treasury bond futures contract is 108.50. Bond X is quoted at 99.00 with a conversion factor of 0.9050; bond Y is quoted at 121.75 with a conversion factor of 1.1150. (a) Which is cheaper to deliver? (b) If bond X is delivered with accrued interest of $0.80 per $100, what is received for one contract?\n(a) X: \\(99.00-108.50\\times0.9050=99.00-98.1925=0.8075\\). Y: \\(121.75-108.50\\times1.1150=121.75-120.9775=0.7725\\). Bond Y is cheaper to deliver. (b) \\(108.50\\times0.9050+0.80=98.9925\\) per $100; \\(98.9925\\times1{,}000=\\$98{,}992.50\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:Exercises", "chapter": 6, "section": "Exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 6, Interest Rate Futures and Forward Rate Agreements — Exercises Exercises", "body": "6.7 A portfolio of bonds is worth $40,000,000 and has a duration of 5.2 years. Bond futures are quoted at 115.00 on $100,000 of principal, and the cheapest-to-deliver bond has a duration of 8.0 years. (a) Find the hedge. (b) How many contracts reduce the duration of the portfolio to 3.0 years?\n\\(V_F=\\$115{,}000\\). (a) \\(N^{*}=(40{,}000{,}000\\times5.2)/(115{,}000\\times8.0)=226.1\\); short 226 contracts. (b) The duration to be removed is \\(5.2-3.0=2.2\\) years: \\((40{,}000{,}000\\times2.2)/(115{,}000\\times8.0)=95.7\\); short 96 contracts.\n6.8 ★ A three-month SOFR futures contract whose reference quarter begins in six years is quoted at 95.20. The standard deviation of the change in the short rate in one year is 1.1 percent. Estimate the forward rate for the reference quarter with continuous compounding, treating the futures rate as continuously compounded.\nFutures rate: 4.80 percent. Adjustment: \\(0.5\\times0.011^{2}\\times6\\times6.25=0.00227\\), or 22.7 basis points. Forward rate: \\(4.80-0.23=4.57\\) percent."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
